EXT. RANCH - BUNKHOUSE PORCH - EVENING

Long shadows. Cicadas. A line of horses at a trough, tails switching. The sun sinks behind a ridge the color of rust.

Five ranch hands sit on the porch. Nobody has said much in an hour.

AMOS PRUITT (60s, foreman, gray beard) smokes a pipe. DELFINA RIVAS (30s, braid, work gloves tucked in her belt) mends a bridle. HANK SORENSEN (40s, big, quiet) peels an apple with a pocketknife. YOUNG TOBIAS (17, hat too big) plays three notes on a harmonica, over and over. And old CLEOPHAS "CLEO" JAMES (70s, one eye clouded) rocks in a battered chair.

The harmonica stops. Tobias studies the horizon.

Delfina ties off a knot. Hank halves the apple and hands a piece to Tobias without a word. Tobias takes it. Amos taps out his pipe against the rail.

Cleo lifts his chin. Sniffs the air.

AMOS
Rain?

CLEO
Maybe.

Neither says anything else. The first star shows in the east.
